        "heading": "2.2 MEASUREMENT ERROR MODEL",
        "text": "In simplest terms, measurement error problems can be described as the problem of making inferences about a statistical model in terms of a variable X that is not directly observable. Instead, a surrogate variable W of X is observed, and inference must be made through W instead. The statistical models and inference methods are called measurement error models (Stefanski, 2000).\nA measurement error model usually consists of two parts, the first is the error structure relating the surrogate W to the truth X , and the second is data structure of true variable X (Carroll et al., 2006).\nIn the majority of the measurement error literature, the error structure of classical measurement error is assumed, in which the true (unobserved) variable X is measured with additive error,\nW = X + U (1)\nwhere the additive error U is usually assumed to have constant variance. There are other types of error structures such as multiplicative error, but in this paper, we will restrict ourselves to only additive measurement error. On the other hand, the data structure is determined by the properties of X . Typically, there is a distinction between the functional model where X is not modelled and the structural model where X is modelled with a parametric distribution. For this paper, we will restrict our attention to structural modelling where X is assumed to have a Gaussian distribution.\nAlthough measurement error problems encompass a large class of problems, their origins lie in the regression setting where the explanatory variable X is unobservable and its surrogate/measurement W can be observed instead. In this case, the error structure about the surrogate W and truth X is assumed to be additive, W = X + U , where the measurement error U is assumed to have mean zero, constant variance and is independent of X . Next, the data structure consists of the assumptions on the marginal distribution of X , which is assumed to have a Gaussian distribution as mentioned above, and on the conditional distribution of the response variable Y given X ,\nY = g(X;\u03b2) + q\nwhere q denotes the regression error and is assumed to have a Gaussian distribution with mean zero, constant variance and is independent of X and U . This model is commonly referred as error in variable model (Fuller, 1987). For g(X;\u03b2) = X\u22a4\u03b2, it reduces to the familiar linear regression model. For g(X;\u03b2) = { 1 + exp ( X\u22a4t \u03b2 )}\u22121 and binary response Y , it reduces to the classical logistic regression model.\nUnder the linear regression setting, one of the most well-known effects of the measurement error is to bias the regression coefficient towards zero. This phenomenon is commonly referred to as attenuation. More precisely, the OLS estimator obtained by regressing Y on the surrogate W is not a consistent estimator of \u03b2 but instead of \u03b2\u2217 = \u03bb\u03b2 where\n\u03bb = \u03c32x\n\u03c32x + \u03c3 2 u\n< 1 (2)\nThe attenuation factor \u03bb is referred as the reliability ratio (Carroll et al., 2006). The larger the \u03c32u, the variance of the measurement error, the closer to zero the attenuation factor \u03bb will be. Therefore, when ignoring the measurement error, the naive method of regressing Y on W will result in severe underestimation of \u03b2 when the magnitude of measurement error is large.\nBased on equation 2, we can obtain a consistent estimator of \u03b2 as\n\u03b2\u0303 = \u03b2\u0302ols/\u03bb\u0302 = \u03b2\u0302ols Sw\nSw \u2212 \u03c3\u03022u (3)\nwhere Sw is the sample variance of the surrogate W and \u03c3\u03022u is a consistent estimator of \u03c3 2 u.\nSo far we have only discussed the scenario where only the explanatory variable X is measured with error, but the scenario where both explanatory and response variables are measured with error is much more beneficial to the differential privacy framework. Although there is admittedly less literature on response measurement error, the extension is surprisingly easy in some cases. As noted in Carroll et al. (2006), for unbiased and homoscedastic response measurement error in linear regression, the response measurement error increases the variability of the fitted lines without causing bias. Furthermore, all hypothesis tests, confidence intervals, etc. remain perfectly valid albeit they are less powerful. These conclusions indicate that unbiased error in linear regression requires no special adjustments when extending to response measurement error. However, for the binary response, such as logistic regression, the measurement error becomes misclassification, which is no longer an unbiased error, and therefore special considerations are required. As the first steps establish the connections between differential privacy and the measurement error model, we will focus on the linear regression measurement error throughout this paper. Nonlinear regression such as logistic regression will be left for future directions."
